course: english
lesson: 45
title: Poetry: rhyme and rhythm
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rhyme is when words end with the same sound, like wall and fall in the old nursery rhyme "Humpty Dumpty sat on a wall, Humpty Dumpty had a great fall"; rhymes click together like puzzle pieces and make lines easy to remember.
explain: Rhythm is the beat of a line, the pattern of strong and weak sounds you can clap: "HICK-o-ry, DICK-o-ry, DOCK, the MOUSE ran UP the CLOCK" has a beat you can march to, just like a drum.
explain: Rhythm can match the meaning: Robert Louis Stevenson's train poem goes "Faster than fairies, faster than witches, / Bridges and houses, hedges and ditches", and the quick beat rattles along like wheels on a track, while a sad poem is read slowly and softly.
explain: A rhyme must still make sense: "The sea is blue, I like a shoe" rhymes, but the shoe wandered in only for the sound; good poets choose the meaning first and hunt for a rhyme that fits it.
explain: Rhyme depends on accent: a Glasgow boy says "'Bath' doesnae rhyme wi' 'laugh' for me, Miss" ("doesnae" is doesn't), because in Scotland and the North bath has a short a, while in much of the South of England it rhymes with laugh; and not every poem rhymes at all, while rap is poetry with a heavy beat.
fact: Rhyme is when words end with the same sound, such as wall and fall, or dock and clock.
fact: Rhyme makes poems easier to remember and fun to say, which is one reason nursery rhymes have lasted hundreds of years.
fact: Rhythm is the pattern of beats in a line, made by strong and weak syllables; clapping helps you feel it.
fact: A quick rhythm can feel exciting or busy, and a slow one can feel calm or sad.
fact: Rhymes can come at the ends of lines or inside a line, as in "Hey diddle diddle, the cat and the fiddle" (internal rhyme).
fact: A rhyme pattern can be shown with letters: lines that rhyme get the same letter, so a verse where lines 1 and 2 rhyme and lines 3 and 4 rhyme is AABB.
fact: Whether two words rhyme can depend on accent: bath and laugh rhyme in much of southern England but not in the North or in Scotland.
fact: Some words have few or no perfect rhymes, such as orange, month and the Australian word arvo (afternoon); poets can use a near rhyme or put the word in the middle of a line.
fact: Free verse is poetry without a regular rhyme or beat; it uses line breaks and images instead.
fact: Rap uses rhythm, rhyme and wordplay, so it is a kind of poetry.
fact: A rhyming dictionary lists words by their sounds; poets have used them for centuries, and choosing the right rhyme is still the poet's job.
fact: "From a Railway Carriage", the train poem, was written by Robert Louis Stevenson and published in 1885; "No cap" in slang means "for real".
exercise: Ruby shouts "Wall and fall sound the same!" What has she noticed? | They rhyme | They have the same number of letters | They start with the same sound | They mean the same thing
exercise: The teacher claps along to "Hickory, dickory, dock". What is the clapping helping children feel? | The rhythm | The rhyme | The spelling | The meaning
exercise: Kai from Manchester writes "I got on the bus, it was full of us." He asks "Is that boss, Miss?" ("Boss" means great.) What is good about it? | The rhyme fits what he means | It rhymes "bus" with "Miss" | It has no rhythm at all, so it is free | It uses a very long word to rhyme
exercise: Freya writes "The sea is blue, I like a shoe." What is the problem? | The shoe is only there for the rhyme | "Blue" and "shoe" do not rhyme in English | The sea is not really blue, it is grey | Poems cannot be about the sea
exercise: Tom from Glasgow says "'Bath' doesnae rhyme wi' 'laugh' for me, Miss." Who is right? | Both: rhyme depends on accent | Only the teacher, as Scottish is wrong | Only Tom, as southern accents are wrong | Nobody, because bath never rhymes
exercise: The teacher reads a galloping poem fast and a sad poem slowly. Why? | Rhythm and speed can match the mood | Sad poems are always much shorter than fast poems | She was running out of time for the lesson | Fast poems must always be read before slow ones
exercise: Jayden, 10, says "Rap is poetry, no cap." His teacher agrees. Why? | Rap uses rhythm, rhyme and wordplay | Rap is written in very old English, like Shakespeare | Rap has no words, only music | Rap always tells a fairy tale with a moral
exercise: In "Hey diddle diddle, the cat and the fiddle", where is the rhyme? | Inside the line: diddle and fiddle | Only at the very ends of the two lines | Between "hey" and "cat" | There is no rhyme in it
exercise: Mum in Sydney says "Write a poem about the arvo." Ella cries "What rhymes with arvo?!" What could she do? | Put "arvo" in the middle of a line | Give up and write about the beach instead | Make up a word that means nothing at all | Spell it "arvoh" so it rhymes
exercise: Leo asks "Isn't a rhyming dictionary cheating?" What is the best answer? | No; poets use them and still choose | Yes; poets must find every rhyme by memory | Yes; it is against the rules of English | No; it writes the whole poem for you
exercise: Which pair rhymes? | clock and dock | cat and dog | mouse and mice | sun and moon
exercise: Clap "TWIN-kle, TWIN-kle, LIT-tle STAR". How many strong beats are there? | Four | Two | Seven | Eight
exercise: A verse ends with the words cat, hat, tree, sea. What is its rhyme pattern? | AABB | ABAB | ABCD | AAAA
exercise: Which poem is most likely free verse? | One with no regular rhyme or beat | One where every line rhymes with "day" | A nursery rhyme with a strong beat | A limerick with five rhyming lines
exercise: Stevenson's train poem has a quick, rattling beat. Why does that suit it? | It sounds like the train rushing along | It makes the poem much easier to spell | Trains are slow and sad, so it is a joke | It hides all of the rhymes from the reader
exercise: Why have nursery rhymes lasted hundreds of years? | Rhyme and rhythm make them easy to remember | They were printed in every school book by law | Children are forced to learn them each day | They were written last year by a famous singer
test: Tom, Gen Alpha, says "Poems are mid, they don't even rhyme sometimes." ("Mid" means not very good.) What is the truth? | Many poems use rhythm and images instead of rhyme | A poem that doesn't rhyme is not a real poem | All poems rhyme if you read them faster | Only bad poets forget to add rhymes
test: ★★★ "All the king's horses and all the king's men / Couldn't put Humpty together again." In which accent do "men" and "again" rhyme? | One where "again" sounds like "a-gen" | One where "men" sounds like "moon" | No accent at all, they never rhyme | Only when you whisper the poem
test: In a cartoon, a poet forces every line to rhyme with "cheese" and the poem ends up about sneezing trees. What lesson does it show? | Rhyme should not push out the meaning | Every poem should be about cheese and trees | Rhymes always improve any poem, whatever they say | Trees really can sneeze in poems
test: What would happen if you read "Faster than fairies, faster than witches" very slowly? | It would lose the rushing feel of the train | It would sound even faster than the train itself | The rhymes would disappear | It would become free verse
test: Your cousin from Cork says "That poem's grand, but it's awful sing-songy." ("Grand" means fine.) What is "sing-songy" about? | A very strong, regular rhythm | Too few words in each line of every verse | No rhythm of any kind | The poem has no title
test: ★★★ Which pair rhymes in almost every English accent? | night and light | bath and laugh | grass and farce | path and math
test: Your brother, dead sarky: "'I love my dog, he is a frog.' Genius, mate." What is he mocking? | A rhyme that makes no sense | A poem with no rhyme | A poem that is too long | A very slow rhythm
test: A girl in Texas says "Y'all, I wrote a rap!" Her teacher says "Clap the beat." What will clapping show? | The rhythm of her lines | How many rhymes she spelled | Whether her rap is too long | Whether the words are slang
test: ★★★ A verse ends with day, moon, play, soon. What is its rhyme pattern? | ABAB | AABB | ABBA | ABCD
test: A Welsh nan says "Lush little poem, cariad." ("Lush" means lovely, "cariad" means love.) The poem is "Rain on the roof, rain on the road, / Rain on the back of a dripping toad." Which words rhyme? | road and toad | rain and roof | back and dripping | roof and road
test: Which line has the most obvious beat to march to? | Left, right, left, right, march along tonight | Sometimes, in the quiet, I think about things | The kettle, which is old, gave up | Perhaps I shall not go
exam: Identify the pair of words constituting a rhyme. | fall and wall | fall and fill | fall and fallen | fall and autumn
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises rhythm in poetry? | The pattern of strong and weak beats within a line | The correspondence of sounds at the ends of words | The number of lines in a stanza | The meaning conveyed by a poem's title
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning rhyme and accent. | Whether two words rhyme may depend on the accent of the speaker | All words rhyme identically in every accent | Accent has no bearing on the sound of words | Only one accent is permitted in poetry
exam: Ascertain the rhyme scheme of a quatrain (a verse of four lines) whose lines terminate in "star", "are", "high" and "sky". | AABB | ABAB | ABBA | ABCD
